@*
    A game's own name, isolated from the layout around it (i18n B2).

    `<bdi>` isolates user-supplied text of unknown direction from a fixed-direction layout — without
    it, an Arabic name and the neutral characters beside it renegotiate order with the count in the
    next cell, and the row comes apart. `dir` keeps the name RTL inside its own box while the row
    stays LTR.

    `lang` gives a screen reader the right voice (so 北大侠客行 is read as Chinese, not attempted
    with English phonetics) and selects between regional Han font forms. Set only where the script
    settles it — see NameScript.

    `translate="no"` is deliberately absent: a game's name is the one game-supplied string a reader
    might legitimately want transliterated, unlike hostnames, codebase strings or protocol acronyms.
*@

@Content(Name)

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The same markup without a component instance for each row of a large listing.</summary>
    public static RenderFragment Content(string name) => @<text>@if (NameScript.IsPlainAscii(name))
        { @name }
        else
        { <bdi lang="@NameScript.LanguageOf(name)" dir="@NameScript.DirectionOf(name)">@name</bdi> }</text>;
}
